d reinterpretation of modern  stoicism that manages to carve out a lot  of room for incorporating creativity  into it so this is you know really  excellent stuff  what did I find problematic about the  work often I have a lot to say I'm not  gonna have very much to say here and  what I have to say is fairly trivial  because the book is you know very good  so the only two things that I have to  point out are these one is that there  are a few in Felicity's of expression  where you know you say well that's kind  of a weird way to say it or you know a  book that's that's from stoic literature  has been translated into English and and  the title that Pyotr is using doesn't  seem to be the title that we usually use  but I think that that can be attributed  to the fact that he's a native Polish  speaker and a second English as a second  language for him but those are pretty  rare in the book this this is more well  written in English than most native  English speakers works are in English so  that's really a testament that his his  capacities the other thing is a mere  form a thing you know the the various  themes he there's no page break that's  just you know one after another after  another and it's rather dense I think it  would have been nice to have page breaks  in there but that's that's a purely  preferential thing and you can easily  read the book just as it is I mean  there's a good table of contents there's  an amazing index there's great footnotes  so really I don't have much to say by  way of criticism of this book  final thoughts so this is a great book I  think that you need to get it if you're  interested in either of the two themes  that he's bringing together here  stoicism or artistic creativity I am  actually considering using this as a  text in a philosophical aesthetics class  it's that good and if you're interested  in modern stoicism I think this is a  must have quite frankly so I'll say this  too by way of final thoughts I only read  this this last month as I was preparing  for this Peter had it sent to me when it  first appeared in 2019 I put it into the  stack of books to review and thought  well that's I'm sure it's going to be  good this book is really just simply  excellent and as I said in the beginning  it's the sort of thing that I wish I had  written myself what Aristotle calls  jealous or emulation the pain that you  feel in seeing somebody else doing such  great work and wishing that could be you  instead doing that and enjoying the the  fact of having done that  that's this book it's that good so I  don't think I need to say much more  other than that to wrap this up it's  highly recommended  you
 

